  "why": "The paper studies error-triggering inputs (ETIs) and proposes EP-oriented testing and fine-tuning to uncover and repair transformation-specific DNN errors, but it neither designs its failure oracle, mutation, or exploration to target existing safeguards (e.g., adversarially-trained models, safety-aligned LLMs, or adversarial detectors) nor evaluates against defended/safeguarded models. The experiments and proposed regulations focus on discovering erroneous properties (EPs) across many input transformations and on avoiding ‘‘trapped testing’’, not on bypassing or defeating deployed defenses. No empirical results show bypasses of safeguards or use feedback from safeguards to guide mutation/exploration.",
  "evidence": "Page 1607, Section 2.3 DNN Fine-Tuning: \"The basis of fine-tuning is adapted from the adversarial/robust training [57]. ... This approach is commonly adopted in DNN testing works [33, 40, 59, 68, 69].\" (shows they build on adversarial-training ideas but does not claim targeting defenses)\n\nPage 1606, (end of Introduction / Contributions): \"To address our unveiled issues, we propose a technique that automatically generates a great number of different input transformations... We revisit existing ETI-oriented testing and advocate a new, EP-oriented scheme ... We design simple yet effective EP-oriented regulations, delivering more comprehensive and accurate DNN testing and fine-tuning.\" (demonstrates focus on EP discovery and regulation, not on safeguards)\n\nPage 1610, Section 6 Objectives: \"We consider a broad set of white-box and black-box testing objectives that are widely adopted in different scenarios... Coverage: ... Adequacy: ... Entropy: ...\" (lists typical testing objectives; no mention of evaluating or bypassing safeguards such as adversarially trained models, detectors, or safety-aligned LLMs)\n\nPage 1613, Section 8 (RQ2 Fine-Tuning) opening: \"Fine-tuning with ETIs is previously believed an effective approach to repairing the tested DNN's mis-predictions. Nevertheless, a counter-example is that, if two regions ... enhancing the resilience towards one transformation will presumably make the DNN less resilient to another one.\" (analysis of mutual exclusivity in fine-tuning effects, but not an exploration of bypassing deployed safeguards)\n\n(Throughout the paper’s design and evaluation sections there is no description of: 1) incorporating safeguard outputs/feedback into the fuzzer's oracle or mutation strategy; 2) targeting defended/adversarially-trained models or detectors; or 3) experiments demonstrating that the fuzzer can bypass such safeguards.)"
